# 103. Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order Traversal

## APPROACH: ZIGZAG LEVEL ORDER TRAVERSAL (BFS + DIRECTION FLAG)

Idea:
Standard Level Order Traversal (BFS) hi follow karna hai, bas alternate levels par traversal ka direction badalna hai:
- Level 0 (Even level): Left to Right
- Level 1 (Odd level): Right to Left
- Level 2 (Even level): Left to Right ... and so on.
Ek boolean `flag` maintain karo jo har level complete hone ke baad flip (`flag = !flag`) hoga.

--------------------------------------------------

APPROACH (Iterative / BFS using Queue)

1. Edge Case:
   Agar `root == null` ho, toh seedha empty list return kar do.

2. Ek Queue (`Queue<TreeNode> q`) initialize karo aur `root` ko offer karo.
   Direction control ke liye `boolean flag = true` set karo (`true` = Left to Right, `false` = Right to Left).

3. Jab tak queue empty na ho jaye (`while(!q.isEmpty())`):
   - Current level ka size lock karo: `int levelSize = q.size()`.
   - Temporary list banao: `List<Integer> currLevel = new ArrayList<>()`.

4. Loop chalao `levelSize` times (`for(int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++)`):
   - Node pop karo: `TreeNode currNode = q.poll()`.
   - **Direction Handling:**
     - Agar `flag == true` (L -> R): element ko end me add karo (`currLevel.add(currNode.val)`).
     - Agar `flag == false` (R -> L): element ko 0-th index par daalo (`currLevel.add(0, currNode.val)`).
   - Queue me children ka push order hamesha same rahega:
     - Pehle `currNode.left` (agar non-null ho).
     - Fir `currNode.right` (agar non-null ho).

5. `currLevel` ko final `result` list me add karo.
   Direction flip karo: `flag = !flag`.

6. Final `result` return kar do.

--------------------------------------------------

EXAMPLE

Tree:
        3
       / \
      9   20
          /  \
         15   7

Dry Run:
- Initial: q = [3], flag = true, result = []

Level 1 (flag = true -> L to R):
- levelSize = 1
- Poll 3 -> flag is true -> currLevel.add(3) -> currLevel = [3]
- Queue me 9 aur 20 gaye -> q = [9, 20]
- result = [[3]], flag = false

Level 2 (flag = false -> R to L):
- levelSize = 2
- Poll 9 -> flag is false -> currLevel.add(0, 9) -> currLevel = [9]
- Poll 20 -> flag is false -> currLevel.add(0, 20) -> currLevel = [20, 9]
- Queue me 15 aur 7 gaye -> q = [15, 7]
- result = [[3], [20, 9]], flag = true

Level 3 (flag = true -> L to R):
- levelSize = 2
- Poll 15 -> flag is true -> currLevel.add(15) -> currLevel = [15]
- Poll 7 -> flag is true -> currLevel.add(7) -> currLevel = [15, 7]
- result = [[3], [20, 9], [15, 7]], flag = false

Result: [[3], [20, 9], [15, 7]]

--------------------------------------------------

WHY KEEP QUEUE PUSH ORDER UNCHANGED?

- Queue hamesha actual tree structure ko level-by-level (left-to-right) naturally traverse karti rahegi.
- Direction reversal sirf value store karte waqt list me handle kiya jata hai (`add` vs `add(0, ...)` ya `LinkedList.addFirst()`), jisse queue ka sequence kabhi disturb nahi hota.

--------------------------------------------------

COMPLEXITY

N = Total number of nodes in tree
W = Maximum width of tree (nodes at a level)

Time:
O(N) -> Har node queue me exactly ek baar add aur remove hoti hai.
*(Note: ArrayList me `add(0, val)` $O(K)$ time leta hai shifting ki wajah se. Isko fully $O(1)$ karne ke liye `LinkedList` ya fixed-size array / two pointers use kar sakte hain).*

Space:
O(N) ya O(W) -> Queue me at most widest level ke nodes aate hain (worst case balanced tree me ~N/2 nodes).
*(Excluding output list result)*

--------------------------------------------------

INTERVIEW POINT

- **Optimization discussion:**
  `currLevel.add(0, currNode.val)` ArrayList me elements ko shift karta hai.
  Interviewer ko optimize karke bata sakte ho:
  1. `LinkedList<Integer>` use karo aur `addFirst()` call karo ($O(1)$ insertion at front).
  2. Ya fixed-size array `int[] level = new int[levelSize]` banao aur index calculate karo:
     - L to R: `index = i`
     - R to L: `index = levelSize - 1 - i`
- Core base problem: Binary Tree Level Order Traversal (LeetCode 102).

--------------------------------------------------

# CODE:

import java.util.*;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode() {}
 *     TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) {
            return result;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);
        boolean flag = true; // true: L -> R, false: R -> L

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int levelSize = q.size();
            List<Integer> currLevel = new ArrayList<>();
            
            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
                TreeNode currNode = q.poll();

                // Direction ke basis par insertion position decide karo
                if (flag) {
                    currLevel.add(currNode.val); // Append at end
                } else {
                    currLevel.add(0, currNode.val); // Insert at start
                }

                // Children hamesha Left -> Right order me hi queue me jayenge
                if (currNode.left != null) {
                    q.offer(currNode.left);
                }
                if (currNode.right != null) {
                    q.offer(currNode.right);
                }
            }

            result.add(currLevel);
            flag = !flag; // Direction flip karo next level ke liye
        }

        return result;        
    }
}
